    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-49723",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition",
      "problem": "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7x}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{C}{x-2},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$",
      "worked_solution": "We multiply both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+3)(x-2)$ and get\n\n$$\n7x=(Ax+B)(x-2)+C({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(2) & =(A(2)+B)(2-2)+C({2}^{2}+3) \\\\ 14 & =7C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0) & =(A(0)+B)(0-2)+(2)({0}^{2}+3) \\\\ 0 & =-2B+6 \\\\ 2B & =6 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1) & =(A(1)+3)(1-2)+(2)({1}^{2}+3) \\\\ 7 & =(A+3)(-1)+8 \\\\ 7 & =-A+5 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-2,$ $B=3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\nA+B+C=-2+3+2=3.\n$$",
