    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-102561",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions",
      "problem": "Express $\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.",
      "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+6$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)$ and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2x-3=(Ax+B)(x+5)+C({x}^{2}+6).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-5)^{2}+2(-5)-3 & =(A(-5)+B)(-5+5)+C((-5)^{2}+6) \\\\ 75-10-3 & =31C \\\\ C & =\\frac{62}{31} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}+2(0)-3 & =(A(0)+B)(0+5)+C((0)^{2}+6) \\\\ -3 & =5B+6C \\\\ -3 & =5B+6(2) \\\\ 5B & =-3-12 \\\\ B & =\\frac{-15}{5} \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A$ we choose $x=1$, and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}+2(1)-3 & =(A(1)+B)(1+5)+C((1)^{2}+6) \\\\ 2 & =6A+6B+7C \\\\ 2 & =6A+6(-3)+7(2) \\\\ 6A & =2+18-14 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1,B=-3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)}=\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}.\n$$",
