      "math_academy_id": "q-102571",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions",
      "problem": "Express $\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x-1}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.",
      "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x-1}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}-2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+7x-1=(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}-2x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)^{2}+7(-1)-1 & =(A(-1)+B)(-1+1)+C((-1)^{2}-2(-1)+3) \\\\ -6 & =6C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+7(0)-1 & =(A(0)+B)(0+1)+C({0}^{2}-2(0)+3) \\\\ -1 & =B+3C \\\\ -1 & =B+3(-1) \\\\ B & =3-1 \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+7(1)-1 & =(A(1)+B)(1+1)+C((1)^{2}-2(1)+3) \\\\ 8 & =2A+2B+2C \\\\ 8 & =2A+2(2)+2(-1) \\\\ 2A & =8-4+2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=2,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x-1}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{3x+2}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)}.\n$$",
